Q: How to create a left-packed vector of indices of the 0s in one SIMD vector? A: <p>I was revisiting this problem again - this time just with the problem of compressing 4 floats; but I think the algorithm might generalise somewhat.</p>
<p>Problem statement: partition Convex Hull points to 'left', 'right' and 'discard' -- that's when we have a mask of say<br />
left =  <code>0xffffffff 0x00000000 0x00000000 0xffffffff</code> ;<br />
right = <code>0x00000000 0x00000000 0xffffffff 0x00000000</code> , where the masks are mutually exclusive.</p>
<p>Anyway, for a single mask it holds that the 3 first values out of 4 solely determine a scheduling or a rule where the lanes 0..3 can possibly draw the input.</p>
<pre><code> 
        //   3 2 1 0     lane3 lane2 lane1 lane0
        //   x 0 0 0       3     3     3     3
        //   x 0 0 1       3     3     3     0
        //   x 0 1 0       3     3     3     1
        //   x 0 1 1       3     3     1     0
        //   x 1 0 0       3     3     3     2
        //   x 1 0 1       3     3     2     0
        //   x 1 1 0       3     3     2     1
        //   x 1 1 1       3     2     1     0
</code></pre>
<p>This 3-bit value then encodes 4 Lookup-tables, each of size 2^3 * 2 = 16 bits.</p>
<p>Having 32-bit masks, I ended up duplicating this 16-bit LUT in two adjacent 16-bit lanes, then I shift this left by (0:7) *2 bits, which is computed by a horizontal sum of<br />
<code>hsum = horizontal_sum(masks &amp; [2 4 8 0])</code></p>
<pre><code>        constexpr uint32_t mag3 = 0xffff'ffff;
        constexpr uint32_t mag2 = 0xfffe'fffe;
        constexpr uint32_t mag1 = 0xfde9'fde9;
        constexpr uint32_t mag0 = 0xc484'c484;
</code></pre>
<p>We can then shift the magic vector <code>mag0 mag1 mag2 mag3</code> left by <code>hsum</code> -- which is available both in SSSE3 (without reverting to lane wise variadic shift, although that might be more efficient since <code>phaddd</code> decodes to multiple uops on every x86 micro-architecture) and also in webassembly and ARM instruction set; having strictly positive weights works also for Intel, where as the variadic shift in ARM could benefit from a permutation where the target position is at bits 2:3.</p>
<pre><code>// the same shift for all the 4 lanes
auto magic = Vector4int(mag0, mag1, mag2, mag3) &lt;&lt; hsum;
</code></pre>
<p>Having just 4 values to pick from, I chose to replicate, but with <code>uint16_t data[8]</code> it's better not to.<br />
The vector wide shift left (Intel would have separate instruction for right shift, where as ARM would interpret negative values as right shifts) we bring the <em>lane to draw from</em> at bits 14:15 of each uint16_t. (The total shift is 2 * (0:7), thus we just selected the bits 2,4,and 8 from the masks to avoid this multiplication).</p>
<pre><code>magic &amp;= 0xc000c000; // this works splendidly with gnu vector extensions...
</code></pre>
<p>isolates these top bits, and we can then replicate it in ARM64 by <code>vsra q0, q0, #8</code> or <code>vsri q0, q0, #8</code> or possibly even with some vtrn, zip, uzip instruction.</p>
<p>Then we do <code>magic = (magic &gt;&gt; 4) + 0x03020100</code> to come up with a look up table entry to permute a vector of 4 elements.</p>
<pre><code>compressed = _mm_shuffle_epi8(points, magic);
// or compressed = vqtbl1q_u8(points, magic); // ARM64
</code></pre>
<p>For 2D-points it's best to separate the X,Y to different registers, then apply the the <code>shufb</code> - and for ARM64 3D-points and 4D-points can be easily interleaved by <code>vstN {q0 - qN_1}, [x0], x1</code> .</p>
<p>Now the generalisations for 8-way compress (of uint8_t) would be merely to apply this procedure separately for the bottom 4 and the top 4 elements. Simultaneously one needs to compute the <code>sum(mask)</code> so one can advance the write pointer(s). And if we think about the 8-way compress, then</p>
<pre><code>    mask0 mask1 mask2 mask3 | mask4 mask5 mask6 mask7
    &lt;---- left -----------&gt;   &lt;------ right --------&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>we need to compute the same algorithm separately for the left/right side, but add the value of <code>mask0 + mask1 + mask2 + mask3</code> to the indices where we draw in the values for lanes 4..7.</p>
<ul>
<li>My particular case where I have two mutually exclusive masks is also quite interesting</li>
</ul>
<p>We can fill a single vector with one part filled to the left, and the other part filled to the right:</p>
<pre><code>   auto mag_L = Vector4int(mag0, mag1, mag2, mag3) &lt;&lt; hsum_left_mask;
   auto mag_R = Vector4int(mag3, mag2, mag1, mag0) &lt;&lt; hsum_right_mask;
   auto common = _mm_min_epu16(mag_L, magR);
</code></pre>
<p>Reverting the lookup table now fills the 'right' side towards the MSB and left side toward LSB.<br />
The &quot;don't care&quot; bits align with the unknown lane 3, which we don't know -- but since these values are mutually exclusive, it's enough to take the minimum (of each uint16_t) to generate a lookup table that fills the left values to the left and right values to the right.</p>
<p>This 16-bit LUT is probably the largest one should use, as the next level is 2^7 * 3 bits - and if this is used, then on Intel one can just do the movemask into a 128x16 byte permutation table. This method would anyway allow some recursive generalisation within the SIMD register set (until we need to update the writing pointers by the amount of correct entries.</p>
